{title: Be Thou My Vision}
{artist: Irish traditional, tr. Mary Byrne (1905), versified by Eleanor Hull (1912)}
{key: D}
{time: 3/4}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[D]Be Thou my [G]Vision, O [D]Lord of my [A]heart
[Bm]Naught be all [G]else to me, [D]save that Thou [A]art
[G]Thou my best [A]thought, by [Bm]day or by [G]night
[D]Waking or [G]sleeping, Thy [A]presence my [D]light
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[D]Be Thou my [G]Wisdom, and [D]Thou my true [A]Word
[Bm]I ever [G]with Thee and [D]Thou with me, [A]Lord
[G]Thou my great [A]Father, [Bm]I Thy true [G]son
[D]Thou in me [G]dwelling, and [A]I with Thee [D]one
{end_of_verse}
